Final Answer:
Purdue University's bus transportation services will undergo significant changes starting in 2025.
Here are the key details:  1. **CityBus Agreement for 2024-25**:    - For the 2024-25 academic year,
Purdue University trustees approved a one-year contract with CityBus, where Purdue-affiliated riders
will pay $99 per semester for off-campus services. Purdue will subsidize this cost, meaning students
will only pay $25 for the semester pass. On-campus bus routes will remain free.  2. **Transition to
SP Plus**:    - Starting **July 1, 2025**, Purdue will transition to a three-year contract with SP
Plus (SP+), enhancing campus transit services. This service will commence on **August 25, 2025**,
aligning with the first day of fall semester classes. The SP+ service will provide free transit to
students, faculty, and staff with a valid Purdue ID.  3. **Campus Transit Features**:    - SP+ will
leverage technology for improved services, which includes an enhanced mobile app, a more adaptable
fleet, and expanded campus routes. Free rides will be available from **August 14, 2025**. The fleet
will consist of a mixed size of low-floor buses and electric transit vans, and the service will
feature fixed routes and on-demand transit options.  4. **CityBus Service After Transition**:    -
Post-July 2025, CityBus will continue to provide off-campus transit services, maintaining a
relationship with Purdue to ensure students have access to affordable transportation options.  5.
**Updated Shuttle Schedules**:    - Purdue's Campus Connect commuter shuttle service offers shuttles
between the West Lafayette and Indianapolis campuses, adjusting schedules as needed based on demand
and operational efficiencies.  Students and staff should check Purdue’s official channels for
specific schedules, routes, and any further updates as the transition progresses. If you have
specific questions or need assistance, contacting Purdue's transportation services directly is
recommended.   **Warning**: This information is accurate as per the current context but is subject
to change. Always refer to official university communications for the most reliable updates.
